1
00:00:00,263 --> 00:00:04,093
Portion helps people who run earning equipment, like solar canopies,

2
00:00:04,093 --> 00:00:06,934
share revenue with the people who backed them.

3
00:00:06,943 --> 00:00:09,931
It records the agreed terms, splits each deposit,

4
00:00:09,931 --> 00:00:12,804
and lets each participant claim their part.

5
00:00:13,753 --> 00:00:17,143
Without a shared record, operators track payments by hand,

6
00:00:17,143 --> 00:00:19,614
and backers take the numbers on trust.

7
00:00:20,985 --> 00:00:26,134
This is a real asset, listed on the Portaldot testnet as a disclosed test,

8
00:00:26,134 --> 00:00:30,558
now sold out. Holders receive only money actually deposited.

9
00:00:30,818 --> 00:00:32,644
This is not just an interface.

10
00:00:32,644 --> 00:00:37,703
The public Portaldot explorer shows our contract being deployed through Revive.

11
00:00:37,985 --> 00:00:40,394
One signed batch created that asset.

12
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:43,472
It minted the deed NFT, stored its terms hash,

13
00:00:43,472 --> 00:00:48,539
split it into 100 native shares, and listed it in our Solidity contract.

14
00:00:48,818 --> 00:00:52,330
A second batch bought all 100 shares,

15
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:56,471
deposited 1 test pUSD, and claimed 0.995.

16
00:00:56,502 --> 00:00:58,850
One development account played both roles,

17
00:00:58,850 --> 00:01:01,616
and none of this verifies physical equipment.

18
00:01:01,886 --> 00:01:04,371
Anyone can check this without our app.

19
00:01:04,371 --> 00:01:08,752
One command matches the live contract to our source, byte for byte,

20
00:01:08,752 --> 00:01:12,141
and recomputes every amount. All 15 checks pass.

21
00:01:12,753 --> 00:01:15,656
To show separate roles, we enter the sandbox:

22
00:01:15,656 --> 00:01:21,131
Core v2 in the browser, with fictional roles and pretend funds, not on Portaldot.

23
00:01:22,519 --> 00:01:26,406
Buyer A already has a sandbox allocation of 59.7,

24
00:01:26,406 --> 00:01:30,385
from Riverside Solar Canopy, a fictional asset.

25
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,841
The operator pays in 30.

26
00:01:34,841 --> 00:01:38,886
After a small fee, 29.85 goes to holders.

27
00:01:41,618 --> 00:01:48,035
Buyer A holds two thirds of the staked shares, so the deposit adds 19.9.

28
00:01:48,035 --> 00:01:49,889
79.6 is now ready.

29
00:01:49,908 --> 00:01:54,179
Buyer A claims, and the balance rises by exactly that amount.

30
00:01:54,179 --> 00:01:57,859
This is deposited money, not a guaranteed return.

31
00:02:00,968 --> 00:02:03,989
Core v2 adds something the live contract lacks:

32
00:02:03,989 --> 00:02:06,231
purchase money stays in escrow.

33
00:02:06,248 --> 00:02:09,826
The operator requests the next tranche, and tries to release it.

34
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:14,799
The contract refuses. The named reviewer has not approved.

35
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:17,957
The reviewer approves.

36
00:02:17,957 --> 00:02:21,931
After a one-week challenge window, shown as a simulated time skip,

37
00:02:21,931 --> 00:02:23,687
the release goes through.

38
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,364
Core v2 is local and unaudited.

39
00:02:29,364 --> 00:02:33,946
On the live contract, purchase money goes straight to the operator.

40
00:02:34,219 --> 00:02:37,947
Deployed fees are 1% of sales and 0.5% of deposits.

41
00:02:37,947 --> 00:02:40,579
That alone won't sustain a business,

42
00:02:40,579 --> 00:02:44,922
so a paid operator service is proposed, but unvalidated.

43
00:02:45,185 --> 00:02:49,034
Core v1 is live and verifiable, Core v2 runs locally,

44
00:02:49,034 --> 00:02:51,536
and there are no customers yet.

45
00:02:51,548 --> 00:02:55,369
Next, we test this with a real operator, in a pilot.
